    "rendered_prompt": "<|im_start|>system\nYou are a helpful assistant.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\nAt a competition with $N$ players, the number of players given elite status is equal to $2^{1+\\lfloor \\log_{2} (N-1) \\rfloor}-N$. Suppose that $19$ players are given elite status. What is the sum of the two smallest possible values of $N$?\n\nThink step by step inside <think> </think> tags, then give your final answer inside \\boxed{}.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\nTo solve for the sum of the two smallest possible values of \\( N \\) given that 19 players are given elite status, we start with the equation provided:\n\n\\[\n2^{1 + \\lfloor \\log_2 (N-1) \\rfloor} - N = 19\n\\]\n\nFirst, we need to understand the expression \\( 2^{1 + \\lfloor \\log_2 (N-1) \\rfloor} \\). The floor function \\( \\lfloor \\log_2 (N-1) \\rfloor \\) gives the greatest integer less than or equal to \\( \\log_2 (N-1) \\). Let's denote \\( k = \\lfloor \\log_2 (N-1) \\rfloor \\). Then the expression becomes:\n\n\\[\n2^{1 + k}\n\\]\n\nWe need to find \\( N \\) such that:\n\n\\[\n2^{1 + k} - N = 19\n\\]\n\nThis implies:\n\n\\[\nN = 2^{1 + k} - 19\n\\]\n\nSince \\( k \\) is an integer, \\( N \\) must be an integer. We need to find the smallest values of \\( k \\) such that \\( N \\) is a positive integer. Let's start with the smallest possible value of \\( k \\).\n\n### Case \\( k = 4 \\):\n\\[\nN = 2^{1 + 4} - 19 = 2^5 - 19 = 32 - 19 = 13\n\\]\n\nWe need to verify if \\( N = 13 \\) satisfies the original equation:\n\n\\[\n2^{1 + \\lfloor \\log_2 (13-1) \\rfloor} - 13 = 2^{1 + \\lfloor \\log_2 12 \\rfloor} - 13\n\\]\n\nSince \\( \\lfloor \\log_2 12 \\rfloor = 3 \\):\n\n\\[\n2^{1 + 3} - 13 = 2^4 - 13 = 16 - 13 = 3 \\neq 19\n\\]\n\nSo, \\( N = 13 \\) is not a solution.\n\n### Case \\( k = 5 \\):\n\\[\nN = 2^{1 + 5} - 19 = 2^6 - 19 = 64 - 19 = 45\n\\]\n\nWe need to verify if \\( N = 45 \\) satisfies the original equation:\n\n\\[\n2^{1 + \\lfloor \\log_2 (45-1) \\rfloor} - 45 = 2^{1 + \\lfloor \\log_2 44 \\rfloor} - 45\n\\]\n\nSince \\( \\lfloor \\log_2 44 \\rfloor = 5 \\):\n\n\\[\n2^{1 + 5} - 45 = 2^6 - 45 = 64 - 45 = 19\n\\]\n\nSo, \\( N = 45 \\) is a solution.\n\n### Case \\( k = 6 \\):\n\\[\nN = 2^{1 + 6} - 19 = 2^7 - 19 = 128 - 19 = 109\n\\]\n\nWe need to verify if \\( N = 109 \\) satisfies the original equation:\n\n\\[\n2^{1 + \\lfloor \\log_2 (109-1) \\rfloor} - 109 = 2^{1 + \\lfloor \\log_2 108 \\rfloor} - 109\n\\]\n\nSince \\( \\lfloor \\log_2 108 \\rfloor = 6 \\):\n\n\\[\n2^{1 + 6} - 109 = 2^7 - 109 = 128 - 109 = 19\n\\]\n\nSo, \\( N = 109 \\) is a solution.\n\n### Conclusion:\nThe two smallest possible values of \\( N \\) are 45 and 109. The sum of these values is:\n\n\\[\n45 + 109 = 154\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{154}\n\\]<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\nReview the solution above. Identify and correct any errors; if it is correct, retain its answer. Give your final answer inside \\boxed{}.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\n",
